## Task 3: Expanding the Recursion

Adding **another layer of relative clause (clausal) recursion** — a second reduced relative clause modifying a noun inside the already-embedded clause:

> **"Pieces of a diary Paul tore to shreds he found in the attic lay on the ground."**

Here, *he found in the attic* is a second reduced relative clause modifying *shreds*, which is itself inside the relative clause *Paul tore to shreds he found in the attic*, which modifies *diary*:

```
[NP [NP pieces]
    [PP of
        [NP [NP a diary]
            [CP Paul tore to
                [NP [NP shreds]
                    [CP he found in the attic]]]]]]
```

Schematically:

| Layer | Structure |
|---|---|
| 1 (matrix NP) | *pieces* + PP |
| 2 (embedded NP) | *a diary* + **CP₁** (*Paul tore to shreds…*) |
| 3 (embedded NP inside CP₁) | *shreds* + **CP₂** (*he found in the attic*) |

This gives us **two nested reduced relative clauses**, each modifying a noun inside the other — a clear instance of **iterated clausal recursion**.

I'll work through each task systematically.

---

## Task 1: Recursion

Yes, the sentence contains **clausal recursion** — specifically, **nested relative clauses** (a relative clause embedded within another relative clause).

The head noun *worldview* is modified by a relative clause:
> *that the prose Nietzsche wrote expressed*

Within that relative clause, the NP *the prose* is itself modified by a **second (reduced/contact) relative clause**:
> *Nietzsche wrote* (i.e., *that Nietzsche wrote*, with the relative pronoun deleted)

So we have a relative clause inside a relative clause — **CP recursion within NP**:

$$\text{NP}[\text{NP}[\text{the worldview}]\ \text{CP}[\text{that}\ \text{NP}[\text{the prose}\ \text{CP}[\varnothing\ \text{Nietzsche wrote}]]\ \text{expressed}]]$$

