## Task 3: Expanding the Recursion

Adding a **third layer** of relative clause recursion — embedding another relative clause inside *prose*:

> **"The worldview that the prose Nietzsche found the publisher who rejected expressed expressed was unprecedented."**

Breaking it down:

| Layer | Head | Relative clause |
|-------|------|-----------------|
| 1st | *worldview* | *that [the prose [Nietzsche found [the publisher [who rejected ∅]]]] expressed* |
| 2nd | *prose* | *∅ Nietzsche found [the publisher who rejected]* |
| 3rd | *publisher* | *who rejected ∅* |

Formally:

$$\text{NP}\!\left[\text{worldview}\ \text{CP}\!\left[\text{that}\ \text{NP}\!\left[\text{prose}\ \text{CP}\!\left[\varnothing\ \text{Nietzsche found}\ \text{NP}\!\left[\text{publisher}\ \text{CP}[\text{who rejected}]\right]\right]\right]\ \text{expressed}\right]\right]$$

This gives us **three nested relative clauses**, each modifying the head noun of the clause above it — a clear demonstration of the **unbounded** nature of syntactic recursion.
